tients in the ICU. <source>Intensive Care Med</source><year>2001</year>;<volume>27</volume>:<fpage>1592</fpage>-8.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11685299</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref15"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal">Fahimi F, Ariapanah P, Faizi M, Shafaghi B, Namdar R, Ardakani MT. Errors in preparation and administration of intravenous medications in the intensive care unit of a teaching hospital: an observational study. <source>Aust Crit Care</source><year>2008</year>;<volume>21</volume>:<fpage>110</fpage>-6.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18387813</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal">Kopp BJ, Erstad BL, Allen ME, Theodorou AA, Priestley G. Medication errors and adverse drug events in an intensive care unit: direct observation approach for detection. <source>Crit Care Med</source><year>2006</year>;<volume>34</volume>:<fpage>415</fpage>-25.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16424723</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal">Kumar A, Roberts D, Wood KE, Light B, Parrillo JE, Sharma S, et al. Duration of hypotension before initiation of effective antimicrobial therapy is the critical determinant of survival in human septic shock. <source>Crit Care Med</source><year>2006</year>;<volume>34</volume>:<fpage>1589</fpage>-96.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16625125</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal">Valentin A, Bion J. How safe is my intensive care unit? An overview of error causation and prevention. <source>Curr Opin Crit Care</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>13</volume>:<fpage>697</fpage>-702.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17975393</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal">Bracco D, Favre JB, Bissonnette B, Wasserfallen JB, Revelly JP, Ravussin P, et al. Human errors in a multidisciplinary intensive care unit: a 1-year prospective study. <source>Intensive Care Med</source><year>2001</year>;<volume>27</volume>:<fpage>137</fpage>-45.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11280625</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal">Cullen DJ, Sweitzer BJ, Bates DW, Burdick E, Edmondson A, Leape LL. Preventable adverse drug events in hospitalized patients: a comparative study of intensive care and general care units. <source>Crit Care Med</source><year>1997</year>;<volume>25</volume>:<fpage>1289</fpage>-97.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9267940</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal">Webster CS. The nuclear power industry as an alternative analogy for safety in anaesthesia and a novel approach for the conceptualisation of safety goals. <source>Anaesthesia</source><year>2005</year>;<volume>60</volume>:<fpage>1115</fpage>-22.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16229697</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal">Tarnow-Mordi WO, Hau C, Warden A, Shearer AJ. Hospital mortality in relation to staff workload: a 4-year study in an adult intensive-care unit. <source>Lancet</source><year>2000</year>;<volume>356</volume>:<fpage>185</fpage>-9.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10963195</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal">Ream RS, Mackey K, Leet T, Green MC, Andreone TL, Loftis LL, et al. Association of nursing workload and unplanned extubations in a pediatric intensive care unit. <source>Pediatr Crit Care Med</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>8</volume>:<fpage>366</fpage>-71.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17545927</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref24"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal">Hugonnet S, Chevrolet JC, Pittet D. The effect of workload on infection risk in critically ill patients. <source>Crit Care Med</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>35</volume>:<fpage>76</fpage>-81.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17095946</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref25"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal">Tang FI, Sheu SJ, Yu S, Wei IL, Chen CH. Nurses relate the contributing factors involved in medication errors. <source>J Clin Nurs</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>16</volume>:<fpage>447</fpage>-57.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17335520</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref26"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal">McCauley K, Irwin RS. Changing the work environment in ICUs to achieve patient-focused care: the time has come. <source>Chest</source><year>2006</year>;<volume>130</volume>:<fpage>1571</fpage>-8.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17099040</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref27"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal">Zohar D, Livne Y, Tenne-Gazit O, Admi H, Donchin Y. Healthcare climate: a framework for measuring and improving patient safety. <source>Crit Care Med</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>35</volume>:<fpage>1312</fpage>-7.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17414090</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref28"><label>28</label><citation citation-type="journal">Minvielle E, Aegerter P, Dervaux B, Boumendil A, Retbi A, Jars-Guincestre MC, et al. Assessing organizational performance in intensive care units: a French experience. <source>J Crit Care</source><year>2008</year>;<volume>23</volume>:<fpage>236</fpage>-44.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18538217</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref29"><label>29</label><citation citation-type="journal">Beckmann U, Bohringer C, Carless R, Gillies DM, Runciman WB, Wu AW, et al. Evaluation of two methods for quality improvement in intensive care: facilitated incident monitoring and retrospective medical chart review. <source>Crit Care Med</source><year>2003</year>;<volume>31</volume>:<fpage>1006</fpage>-11.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12682464</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref30"><label>30</label><citation citation-type="journal">Vincent C, Stanhope N, Crowley-Murphy M. Reasons for not reporting adverse incidents: an empirical study. <source>J Eval Clin Pract</source>